In all honesty, I feel like the keys to understanding some of the biggest problems we face, plastic pollution, terraforming planets, understanding consciousness, healing individualistic society...are all wrapped up in what we still don't understand about [fungi](#fungi-intelligence "ontological:defines") and related things like slime molds, etc.

Pondered fungi's potential to solve complex global challenges.

There's something profound in that observation. Fungi really do operate at the intersection of so many systems we're trying to understand better.

The plastic pollution angle is already showing real promise - researchers have found fungi that can [break down polyurethane](#plastic-pollution "dynamical:transforms_to"), PET, and other plastics that seemed permanently problematic. Some can even do it in oxygen-poor environments, which opens up possibilities for treating landfills and ocean garbage patches. {#plastic-pollution}

For terraforming, fungi are essentially the planet's [original ecosystem engineers](#terraforming "ontological:defines"). They [break down rock into soil](#terraforming "dynamical:transforms_to"), create the foundation for plant communities, and establish those incredible [mycorrhizal networks](#social-healing "meta:corresponds_to") that let forests share resources and information. Any attempt to make a dead planet habitable would probably need to start with the humble work of decomposition and soil building that fungi excel at. {#terraforming}

The consciousness piece is fascinating because fungal networks seem to exhibit something like [distributed intelligence](#fungi-intelligence "ontological:defines") - making decisions, optimizing pathways, even appearing to learn and remember without having anything we'd recognize as a brain. Slime molds solving maze problems and finding efficient transport routes challenges our assumptions about what intelligence looks like. {#fungi-intelligence}

And yeah, the social healing aspect might be the most important. [Mycorrhizal networks](#social-healing "ontological:defines") are basically nature's proof that [collaboration beats competition](#social-healing "epistemic:supports") - trees sharing carbon, nutrients, and chemical signals through fungal intermediaries, caring for their young and supporting struggling neighbors. It's a living model of [mutual aid](#social-healing "meta:corresponds_to") that directly contradicts the "survival of the fittest" narrative that underlies so much of our individualistic thinking. {#social-healing}

