[Indigenous knowledge keepers](#indigenous-wisdom "ontological:defines") have been observing these patterns for thousands of years, but because they couldn't explain the atmospheric physics of spore nucleation, their observations got dismissed as "superstition." Never mind that they were actually [correct about the relationship](#rain-cycle "epistemic:supports"). {#indigenous-wisdom}

And this has happened over and over - traditional ecological knowledge about forest management, soil health, plant medicine, animal behavior - so much of it is now being [validated by science](#indigenous-wisdom "epistemic:supports"), decades or centuries after being written off. 

It's like Western science spent 400 years being obsessed with taking clocks apart to understand time, while [indigenous wisdom](#indigenous-wisdom "epistemic:supports") was saying "maybe look at how the whole forest breathes together." Both approaches have value, but we've missed so much by privileging one over the other.

Now we're having to painfully reconstruct [systems thinking](#indigenous-wisdom "meta:corresponds_to"), complexity science, ecological relationships - essentially rediscovering what many cultures never forgot in the first place.

